<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Tech and Lifestyle Blog</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/</link><description>Recent content on Tech and Lifestyle Blog</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><copyright>© 2025 Yalchin Mammadli</copyright><lastBuildDate>Tue, 19 Aug 2025 13:36:21 +0200</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>How to Secure Ipfs Node</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-secure-ipfs-node/</link><pubDate>Tue, 19 Aug 2025 13:36:21 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-secure-ipfs-node/</guid><description>&lt;h1 class="relative group">Why You Should Never Expose the IPFS RPC API Publicly (and How to Secure It)
&lt;div id="why-you-should-never-expose-the-ipfs-rpc-api-publicly-and-how-to-secure-it" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#why-you-should-never-expose-the-ipfs-rpc-api-publicly-and-how-to-secure-it" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>When running an &lt;a href="https://ipfs.tech/" target="_blank">IPFS&lt;/a> node, one of the most important security considerations is how you handle &lt;strong>ports&lt;/strong> and the &lt;strong>RPC API&lt;/strong>. Many new users unknowingly expose their node’s RPC API to the internet, leaving it wide open for abuse. In this post, we’ll explore why this is dangerous and how you can protect yourself with IPFS’s built-in &lt;strong>basic authentication&lt;/strong>.&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-secure-ipfs-node/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Sorting Algorithms and Binary Search in Python</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/sorting-algorithms-and-binary-search-in-python/</link><pubDate>Fri, 16 May 2025 21:43:57 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/sorting-algorithms-and-binary-search-in-python/</guid><description>&lt;h2 class="relative group">&lt;strong>Selection Sort&lt;/strong>
&lt;div id="selection-sort" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#selection-sort" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>Selection sort is about selecting the minimum value in an unsorted array and moving it towards the front by comparing. Here is an example code snippet to implement binary search in Python:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-py" data-lang="py">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="k">def&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nf">selection_sort&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">):&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="n">length&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nb">len&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">)&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">for&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">index&lt;/span> &lt;span class="ow">in&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nb">range&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">length&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">-&lt;/span> &lt;span class="mi">1&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">):&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="nb">min&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">index&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">for&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">j&lt;/span> &lt;span class="ow">in&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nb">range&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">index&lt;/span>&lt;span class="o">+&lt;/span> &lt;span class="mi">1&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">length&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">):&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">if&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">j&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">&amp;lt;&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nb">min&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">:&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="nb">min&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">j&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">j&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">],&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">index&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">index&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">],&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">j&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">return&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">list_&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="n">unsorted_list&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">7&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">8&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">9&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">8&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">7&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">6&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">5&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">6&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">7&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">8&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">9&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">8&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">7&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">6&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">5&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">6&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">7&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">8&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">0&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="n">selection_sort&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">unsorted_list&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">)&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>
&lt;h3 class="relative group">&lt;strong>Buble Sort&lt;/strong>
&lt;div id="buble-sort" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#buble-sort" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h3>
&lt;p>The bubble sort algorithm is applied by going through an array of data a number of times and at the same time comparing two adjacent numbers at a time in order to reorder them if there are out of order. Here is an example snippet written in Python:&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/sorting-algorithms-and-binary-search-in-python/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Map Sort Filter Functions in Python</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/map-sort-filter-functions-in-python/</link><pubDate>Fri, 16 May 2025 21:28:34 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/map-sort-filter-functions-in-python/</guid><description>&lt;p>This tutorial will help you to see real example of using map, sort, and filter functions along with lambda functions in Python.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>&lt;strong>Map Function Example&lt;/strong>&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Python&amp;rsquo;s map() is a built-in function that allows you to process and transform all the items in an iterable without using an explicit for loop, a technique commonly known as mapping. map() is useful when you need to apply a transformation function to each item in an iterable and transform them into a new iterable.&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/map-sort-filter-functions-in-python/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Implementing Database Logs or History</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/implementing-database-logs-or-history/</link><pubDate>Fri, 16 May 2025 21:22:57 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/implementing-database-logs-or-history/</guid><description>&lt;h2 class="relative group">Introduction
&lt;div id="introduction" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#introduction" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>In the realm of database management, maintaining an accurate record of changes made to the database structure is paramount. This is where the concept of a database changelog comes into play. A changelog, also known as a record history or audit trail, serves as a chronological log of modifications made to the database schema or data. It plays a crucial role in ensuring data integrity, transparency, and accountability within an organization&amp;rsquo;s data management processes.&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/implementing-database-logs-or-history/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Python Decorators With Function and Decorator Parameters</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/python-decorators-with-function-and-decorator-parameters/</link><pubDate>Fri, 16 May 2025 21:17:13 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/python-decorators-with-function-and-decorator-parameters/</guid><description>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-py" data-lang="py">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="k">def&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nf">modify_multiply_decorator&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="o">*&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">args&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">**&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">kwargs&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">):&lt;/span> &lt;span class="c1"># coeff=3&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">def&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nf">dec&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">multiply_func&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">):&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">def&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nf">inner&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="o">*&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">nums&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">):&lt;/span> &lt;span class="c1"># (2, 290)&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="n">coefficient&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">kwargs&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="s2">&amp;#34;coefficient&amp;#34;&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span> &lt;span class="c1"># 3&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="n">result&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">multiply_func&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="o">*&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">nums&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">)&lt;/span> &lt;span class="c1"># 2 * 290&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="n">modified_result&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">coefficient&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">*&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">result&lt;/span> &lt;span class="c1"># 3 * 580&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">return&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">modified_result&lt;/span> &lt;span class="c1"># 1740&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">return&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">inner&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">return&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">dec&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-py" data-lang="py">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="nd">@modify_multiply_decorator&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">coefficient&lt;/span>&lt;span class="o">=&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">3&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">)&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="k">def&lt;/span> &lt;span class="nf">multiply&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="o">*&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">nums&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">):&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl"> &lt;span class="k">return&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">nums&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">0&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span> &lt;span class="o">*&lt;/span> &lt;span class="n">nums&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">[&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">1&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">]&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="nb">print&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="n">multiply&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">(&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">2&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">,&lt;/span>&lt;span class="mi">290&lt;/span>&lt;span class="p">))&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/python-decorators-with-function-and-decorator-parameters/featured.png"/></item><item><title>How to Setup Mailgun in Django</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-mailgun-in-django/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 May 2025 12:29:36 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-mailgun-in-django/</guid><description>&lt;p>In this tutorial, I will try to cover configuring a custom domain for sending emails in your application. The benefit of using mailgun is that it allows you to send (5,000) emails for free during the first three months (then you will pay $0.8 per 1,000 emails) and its setup procedure is so straightforward. You will only need to add your payment verification to be able to send emails without any restrictions.&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-mailgun-in-django/featured.png"/></item><item><title>How to Setup Github Ssh Key</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-github-ssh-key/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 May 2025 12:27:47 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-github-ssh-key/</guid><description>&lt;p>&lt;strong>Github SSH Key set up&lt;/strong>&lt;/p>
&lt;p>This tutorial will help you to be able to set up SSH key connection to your github account on a &lt;strong>Linux&lt;/strong> machine. You can also continue to read this tutorial if you want to set up &lt;strong>&lt;em>Deploy Keys&lt;/em>&lt;/strong>.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>For now let&amp;rsquo;s just assume that &lt;strong>you&amp;rsquo;ve successfully set up SSH key&lt;/strong>, after that you need to make sure that you don&amp;rsquo;t access your repo with HTTPS, but with ssh, so if you previously cloned your repo wirh https set it to ssh as below:&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-setup-github-ssh-key/featured.png"/></item><item><title>How to Deploy Nodejs App</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-deploy-nodejs-app/</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 May 2025 00:53:10 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-deploy-nodejs-app/</guid><description>&lt;p>In this tutorial, we will be deploying a simple URL shortener API built-in express framework of NodeJs. It is pretty simple, uses MongoDB as its database, and you can find its source code &lt;a href="https://github.com/Yalchin403/express-urlshortener-api" target="_blank">here&lt;/a>.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>As a VPS, I use a Linode ubuntu server, and I assume that you have at least some basic knowledge when it comes to Linux.&lt;/p>
&lt;h3 class="relative group">Install Node and NPM
&lt;div id="install-node-and-npm" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#install-node-and-npm" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h3>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma">&lt;code class="language-sh" data-lang="sh">&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">curl -sL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_12.x &lt;span class="p">|&lt;/span> sudo -E bash -
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">sudo apt install nodejs
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">&lt;span class="c1"># verify that bash is installed&lt;/span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span class="line">&lt;span class="cl">node --version
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>
&lt;h3 class="relative group">Clone your Repo
&lt;div id="clone-your-repo" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#clone-your-repo" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h3>
&lt;p>&lt;code>git clone [link to your github repo]&lt;/code>&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/how-to-deploy-nodejs-app/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Accent and Case Insensitive Search and Minimum Distance Calculator in Pymongo</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/accent-and-case-sensitive-search-pymongo/</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 May 2025 00:51:03 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/accent-and-case-sensitive-search-pymongo/</guid><description>&lt;h1 class="relative group">Ellaborate case
&lt;div id="ellaborate-case" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#ellaborate-case" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>Before starting with set up and everything, I would love to confess that I find Mongodb and Python combination as &lt;strong>pain in the ass&lt;/strong>. Recently I have got an job offer as Python/Flask developer in one of the local companies in my country. They are using Mongodb as their production database. They requested a little bit of task to accomplish before we move on to HR interview. So the task was about coding autocomplete search logic and also finding the closest location to our current location from the database. Task seems interesting and pretty much easy to solve, at least for me as a Django developer who have used Postgresql as default database in most of the cases. However, seemed like things don&amp;rsquo;t go so well with Mongodb in django. It seemed like if Python is envolved, we normally have two pypi packages two use, namely &lt;a href="https://pymongo.readthedocs.io/en/stable/" target="_blank">pymongo&lt;/a> and &lt;a href="https://docs.mongoengine.org/" target="_blank">mongoengine&lt;/a>. I was familiar with pymongo as in my &lt;a href="https://t.me/mp3converteryoutubebot" target="_blank">telegram bot&lt;/a> I use Mongodb as database and use pymongo to implement database actions. Hence, below I will explain how I implemented case and accent insensitive search and minimum distance finding via pymongo.&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/accent-and-case-sensitive-search-pymongo/featured.png"/></item><item><title>Securely Configure Vps</title><link>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/securely-configure-vps/</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 May 2025 00:20:23 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/securely-configure-vps/</guid><description>&lt;p>For your information, this tutorial is not written by me absolutely, I&amp;rsquo;ve used ssh connection part of &lt;a href="https://notthebe.ee/Creating-your-own-OpenVPN-server.html" target="_blank">this&lt;/a> tutorial and added some comments to make individual tutorial on &amp;ldquo;how to create your own vpn&amp;rdquo; and also covers the things that we can implement to make our vps the way more secure.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>&lt;strong>Generating SSH keys&lt;/strong>&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Using a cleartext password to log in to your server is never a good idea since the password is not encrypted in transit and can be exposed on a hostile network. By creating an SSH key we&amp;rsquo;re going to make it so that you can only log in to the server if you have the key file and the password, and at the same time the password is encrypted. If you&amp;rsquo;re using Linux you probably already know how to open the terminal, if you&amp;rsquo;re on Mac you can find the Terminal app in your Applications folder, and on Windows 10 you&amp;rsquo;ll need to open the PowerShell with administrator&amp;rsquo;s privileges and install SSH using this command:&lt;/p></description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://yalchin403.github.io/blog/posts/securely-configure-vps/featured.png"/></item></channel></rss>